What the lab would look like

The data layer is where archaeology is rich and underexploited. There are LiDAR scans of forests and deserts, decades of satellite imagery, tens of thousands of radiocarbon dates, ancient DNA from thousands of individuals, museum collections nobody has fully catalogued, and hundreds of thousands of cuneiform tablets that have never been translated. There's also a mountain of "grey literature," excavation reports sitting in regional archives that no human has cross-referenced.

The agent layer would do what the biology agents did: scan everything for anomalies. That could mean a date that doesn't fit its layer, a shape in LiDAR that looks built rather than natural, a genetic signal implying a migration nobody has proposed, or two excavation reports on different continents describing the same unusual technique.

The "wet lab" equivalent is the field team: people who go dig a test trench, get a new radiocarbon date, or have a specialist translate a flagged tablet. That's the part that turns a pattern into a discovery.

This isn't hypothetical. AI-assisted surveys have already turned up hundreds of new Nazca geoglyphs, LiDAR revealed entire lost Maya cities, and machine learning is reading carbonized Herculaneum scrolls that were unreadable for 2,000 years.

On Hancock

You're partly right that something has been off. The mainstream has been badly wrong about timelines before. Göbekli Tepe pushed monumental building back to around 9500 BCE, and the White Sands footprints put humans in North America 21,000+ years ago, overturning the "Clovis first" consensus. An AI lab would likely find more surprises like that, because timelines keep getting older as evidence improves.

But Hancock's specific claim, an advanced global civilization wiped out in the Ice Age, is different, and a real lab would have to test it rather than assume it. The useful move is to ask what such a civilization would have left behind that's hard to erase: metal-smelting pollution in Greenland ice cores (Roman mining shows up clearly, so older industry should too), genetic traces of domesticated crops and animals, quarry and mining scars, and garbage (every human society leaves trash). So far those signatures aren't there, which is why most archaeologists reject the idea. An AI lab could run those checks far more exhaustively than anyone has. If it found something, that would be huge. If it didn't, that would be an answer too.

The key design rule is what separates a discovery lab from a belief machine. You write down in advance what evidence would confirm or kill a hypothesis, and you include "skeptic" agents whose job is to attack every finding before it goes to the field team. That's what made the biology result credible: the pattern was real before anyone got excited about it.

If you wanted to start small, the most promising first projects are probably mass-scanning open LiDAR and satellite data for unrecorded sites, and cross-referencing global radiocarbon databases for dates that don't fit the current story. Those are exactly the places where "we've got the timeline wrong" discoveries tend to come from.

Examining claims about controversial human origins evidence.
Examining claims about controversial human origins evidence.
Yes, I think there's a genuinely good idea in there, though not quite the one the book intended.

A bit of context on the book first. Forbidden Archaeology (Cremo and Thompson, 1993) argued that anatomically modern humans existed millions of years ago, and that archaeologists had filtered out the evidence. The authors were open about their motivation: they were affiliated with a Hare Krishna institute and were looking for support for Vedic accounts of extreme human antiquity. Most of their anomalies are 19th and early 20th century finds with poor records, like a skull reported in a mine shaft or a tool supposedly found in ancient rock, where you can no longer check how it got there. So the catalog itself is weak data.

But their central observation has something to it. Science does filter anomalies, sometimes wrongly. Monte Verde in Chile was dismissed for about twenty years before being accepted as a 14,500-year-old site that broke the "Clovis first" model. Jebel Irhoud in Morocco pushed Homo sapiens back to around 300,000 years. Denisovans were discovered from a finger bone's DNA, and nobody had predicted them. Other anomalies rightly died: the Calico Hills "tools" in California, for instance, are now widely accepted as naturally broken rocks.

That points to the project I'd actually start with: study the anomalies themselves as a dataset.

Step one is to build a catalog of historical archaeological anomalies whose fate is now known, both the ones that turned out real (Monte Verde, White Sands, Jebel Irhoud) and the ones that fell apart (Calico, Piltdown, various contamination cases). Agents can assemble this from published literature, noting for each: what was claimed, what the objections were, what evidence settled it, and how long that took.

Step two is to look for patterns in what separates the real anomalies from the false ones. Was it multiple independent dating methods agreeing? Clean stratigraphy? Replication at a second site? Specific types of objection that turned out to be bias rather than evidence? This would be a genuinely new contribution. As far as I know, nobody has done this systematically, and it would interest both mainstream archaeologists and philosophers of science.

Step three is to apply those lessons to anomalies that are open right now. There are plenty: the Cerutti Mastodon site in California (claimed at 130,000 years, heavily disputed), Pedra Furada in Brazil, various early dates in the Americas and Australia. The lab could score each on how closely it resembles past anomalies that turned out real. That gives you a ranked list of "these deserve a serious second look," which is exactly the kind of lead a field archaeologist would want.

The radiocarbon version fits in neatly as a parallel track. In the big date databases, agents flag every date that disagrees with its site's accepted chronology, then work through the boring explanations first: contamination, "old wood" (burning a centuries-old tree), marine reservoir effects, lab errors, layers getting mixed. Most anomalies will get explained away, and that's fine. Whatever's left after the mundane causes are ruled out is the interesting residue.

The key difference from Forbidden Archaeology is that the lab checks the anomalies as hard as the mainstream checks the orthodoxy. Cremo collected anomalies that supported his view. Your lab would test all of them evenhandedly, and that's what would make its findings hard to dismiss if something real turns up.

The anomaly-fates catalog is also a nice first project because it's pure literature work: no special data access, no GIS skills, and it's the kind of thing agents do well. If you want, I could sketch the catalog's structure, meaning the fields each entry would have and a starter list of 20–30 well-documented cases on both sides.

