Compiling real archaeological cases for a starter catalog.
Here's a name, and a seed file to go with it. I'd call it Residue, after the idea at the heart of the project: rule out the boring explanations first, and whatever's left over is what's interesting. Some alternatives if that doesn't grab you: Second Look, Outlier Atlas, or Palimpsest (a manuscript scraped and rewritten, with older text still showing through).

The file is written as a CLAUDE.md, which Claude Code reads automatically at the start of every session in that folder, so it will always know the mission and rules.

Created a file, shared files
To get going, make a folder called residue, put this file in it, open a terminal there and run claude. A good first message would be: "Read CLAUDE.md, set up the directory structure and schema, then research and draft records for Monte Verde and Piltdown Man as a test." Starting with one vindicated and one refuted case lets you check the records are accurate and useful before scaling up.

One note: I wrote the starter case list from memory, so the file tells Claude Code to verify every detail against real sources rather than trusting it. That rule matters most for the whole project, since the lab is only as credible as its citations.


