Here are some more, grouped by the feeling they give off:

Rooted in archaeology

Stratum: the layers archaeologists dig through; suggests going deeper.
Palimpsest: a manuscript scraped clean and rewritten, with older text still bleeding through. Very fitting for "history that's been overwritten."
Trowel: the archaeologist's basic tool; humble, hands-on, memorable.
Midden: an ancient trash heap, where archaeologists often find the best evidence. Quirky and a bit of an in-joke.
Sherd: a broken piece of pottery; small fragments that reveal whole stories.
About anomalies and rethinking

Outlier: plain and clear about what the project hunts for.
Second Look: friendly, and signals fairness rather than conspiracy.
Reopened: like a cold case file.
Dissent: bolder, maybe too combative for winning over academics.
Evocative and a bit mythic

Deep Time: the geologist's term for vast stretches of the past.
Lodestone: a natural magnet that points the way.
Rosetta: after the stone that unlocked Egyptian hieroglyphs, though it's used by a lot of other projects already.
Cold Case: frames the whole thing as detective work on the past.
My favorites alongside Residue would be Palimpsest (the most poetic and exactly on theme) and Second Look (the most approachable, and the one least likely to make a skeptical archaeologist roll their eyes when you email them). If you pick a different name, just swap it into the title line of the CLAUDE.md; nothing else depends on it.
